<Layout title="Big Data on Little People">
  <h1 data-reveal>Big Data on Little People: Towards Early Skill Assessment at Scale in Resource-poor Field Settings</h1>
  <p class="entry-meta" data-reveal><span>Research</span><span>Submitted to Field Methods, under review</span></p>
  <p class="grey-italics" data-reveal>Research done under the mentorship of Dorien Emmers and Scott Rozelle</p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    The Bayley-III is widely treated as a gold-standard early childhood development assessment, but it is too long, expensive, and labor-intensive for large-scale use in rural communities. 
    This project asked: Can we reduce the Bayley-III to a much shorter assessment while preserving enough validity to make it useful for population-level screening and intervention evaluation?
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    Early developmental delays are common in low-resource settings, but measuring them reliably is difficult. 
    A shorter, validated assessment could make it easier for researchers and policymakers to identify developmental risk, evaluate interventions, and allocate support more efficiently.
  </p>

  <h2>Methods</h2>
  <p>I worked on developing and validating a shortened Chinese version of the Bayley-III using data from 1,173 children under age 3.5 in rural China. The analysis combined:</p>
  <p>Exploratory Factor Analysis to identify latent developmental domains and remove weak or cross-loading items.</p>
  <p>Item Response Theory to select items with strong discrimination power.</p>
  <p>Confirmatory Factor Analysis and correlation analyses to test whether the shortened forms retained validity against full Bayley-III scores and other early childhood development measures.</p>

<h2>Results</h2>
  <p>The final short form reduced the assessment from hundreds of items to a much smaller item set while maintaining meaningful relationships with full Bayley-III outcomes. The strongest tradeoff was a 36-item version, which substantially reduced testing burden while preserving concurrent validity with the original assessment.</p>
  <Figure src="/images/research/latent-dynamics-adaptive-decision-networks/bigdata_figures.svg"
               alt="Foraging task structure"/>

<h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>This project was my first time understanding the idea that “more data” is not automatically better. The hard part was not just building a shorter test, it was deciding what information could be safely discarded. 
  I learned that model fit, validity, and practical usability can point in different directions, and good research often means making that tradeoff explicit rather than pretending there is one perfect metric.</p>

</Layout>
